<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jigonhsasee</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jikonhsaseh, Jikonsase, Tsiokonsaseh, Mother of Nations</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jigonhsasee was an Iroquoian woman considered, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Great Peacemaker</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>, a co-founder of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, dated by some between 1142 and 1450 and by others to about 1570–1600. According to Haudenosaunee oral tradition, she lived beside the warriors' path and gave warriors of every faction food and counsel at her hearth. She joined the Peacemaker's vision of a league in the form of a longhouse, and at the peace gathering he gave her the task of assigning men to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> and gave women the power to choose the chiefs. Called the Mother of Nations, she became the Head Clan Mother, a name passed to her successors until the 1800s.</p></div>
